# mm.audio.microphone

A microphone input, digitised by the platform's ADC. It contains no vendor
header and no board pin number: a provider supplies the channel and this module
drives the protocol.

A microphone is an analog source on an ADC channel. capture fills the caller's
span with sixteen-bit samples: each conversion is centred on the ADC's half
scale, so a quiet input is a sample near zero rather than a large positive
count, then scaled to sixteen bits. count is the number captured, and nothing
being audible is not an error, because a span of any size is a valid capture.

The ADC's conversion rate bounds real-time capture; that is a hardware
qualification, as the touch drivers treat their panel behaviour, and this module
does not promise a rate the ADC cannot deliver.
